# Meridian — Whoop Data Handling Compliance Addendum
**Author:** privacy-compliance
**Date:** 2026-04-19
**Roadmap task:** P1-22
**Trigger:** Whoop OAuth integration (P1-19) shipping

---

## Scope

This document covers the privacy and compliance changes required when WhoopProvider.js ships and Whoop OAuth becomes a live data source. It supplements the existing audit at `Agents/privacy-compliance/audit-report.md` and documents what must be updated in the existing privacy policy.

---

## 1. Scope Justification — Minimum Necessary Data

Scopes requested by WhoopOAuthService.js:

| Scope | Data accessed | Needed? |
|-------|-------------|---------|
| read:recovery | Recovery score, HRV, strain | Yes — core dashboard metric |
| read:sleep | Sleep performance, duration, stages | Yes — core dashboard metric |
| read:workout | Workout strain, heart rate during exercise | Yes — completes the activity picture |
| read:profile | Name, email, DOB, gender | Partial — DOB and gender needed for TDEE; email is NOT used |
| read:body_measurement | Height, weight, max HR | Yes — used for TDEE |
| offline | Refresh token access | Yes — required for auto-refresh |

**Action item:** The `read:profile` scope returns the user's email from Whoop. Meridian does not use it. Verify that WhoopProvider.js does not persist the email to AsyncStorage or any log. If it does, the App Store Privacy Nutrition Label must add a "Contact Info" category. Current implementation does not fetch the profile endpoint — confirm this remains true.

---

## 2. Privacy Policy — Required Updates

The live policy at https://kylefeller14.github.io/Meridian-app-Privacy/privacy-policy.html must be updated before Whoop OAuth ships publicly.

### Add to "Data Sources" section:

> **Whoop:** If you connect your Whoop account, Meridian requests permission to access your recovery score, sleep performance, HRV, workout strain, and body measurements. This data is fetched directly from the Whoop API using OAuth 2.0 and stored on your device. Meridian does not share your Whoop data with any third party. Your Whoop access token is stored in device storage (AsyncStorage in Phase 1; encrypted storage in Phase 2). You can disconnect Whoop at any time from the Settings screen, which removes the stored tokens from your device.

### Add to "Third-Party Services" section:

> **Whoop API:** Meridian connects to the Whoop Health API to retrieve your fitness and recovery data. When you connect your Whoop account, requests are made directly from your device to Whoop's servers. Whoop's own privacy policy governs how Whoop stores and processes your data on their end. Meridian does not receive or store your Whoop credentials — only the OAuth access token issued by Whoop.

---

## 3. App Store Privacy Nutrition Label

The existing label (`Agents/marketing-lead/privacy-nutrition-label.md`) already declares Health & Fitness data as "collected for App Functionality, not linked to identity, not used for tracking." Whoop data falls under this existing declaration.

**No new categories need to be added** as a result of Whoop integration alone, because:
- No new identifiers are collected (the OAuth token is not a user identifier under Apple's definition)
- No data is sent to external servers by Meridian (data flows from Whoop to Meridian, not the reverse)
- The `read:profile` email must NOT be stored — if it is, "Contact Info" must be added

**Confirm with lead-coder:** WhoopProvider.js does not call `/user/profile` — verify this remains true if the provider is extended.

---

## 4. Token Storage Security

AsyncStorage is unencrypted. Storing the Whoop access token in Phase 1 is acceptable because:
- The token grants read-only access to fitness data
- It does not grant access to the user's Whoop password or financial data
- Physical device access is required to extract it

Phase 2 requirement: P2-05 (token migration to `expo-secure-store`) must be completed before Phase 2 ships. Do not skip.

---

## 5. Disconnect and Data Retention

`clearWhoopTokens()` removes the access token, refresh token, expiry, and code verifier from AsyncStorage on disconnect. This satisfies the user-initiated disconnection requirement.

**Note:** Whoop data already merged into `fitapp_health_YYYY-MM-DD` snapshots is NOT deleted when the user disconnects. This is acceptable for Phase 1. The privacy policy should acknowledge: "Disconnecting Whoop removes your connection credentials. Historical fitness data already recorded in the app is retained until you delete the app or request account deletion."

